all right let's get into audience  questions Thanksgiving edition first one  is from Ray ray says hey David in the  context of Harvey Weinstein I was at  Harvey Fierstein not at all who is  accused of anything having done anything  wrong Harvey Weinstein Donald Trump Bill  Cosby Roy Moore Al Franken should the  statute of limitations on sex crimes be  abolished this is an interesting  question so first of all the statutes of  limitations on sex crimes vary there's a  patchwork of laws depending on state to  state sometimes there are exceptions  when it is child sex crimes versus sex  crimes against adults and the argument  for abolishing the statute of  limitations would be listen can take a  long time for victims to be ready to  come forward there's huge stigma and  also it can take a long time to identify  the perpetrator so that would be a  reason to extend the statute of  limitations on sex crimes the  counterpoint to that a general one which  applies to many statutes of limitations  would be listen over time evidence  becomes degraded this includes physical  evidence this includes people's memories  right eyewitness accounts and you need  some kind of a limited amount of time  for a case to be put together it just  can't be indefinite the system needs to  work efficiently and there needs to be  something that will drive the the victim  and the justice system to move more  quickly so what do I think I don't  believe that the statute of limitations  should be abolished on sex crimes but I  am up for the idea of extending it I  think that there is certainly an  argument to be made there but I think  that really the an area that needs to be  looked at is tackling other parts of the  problem right which is number one people  not feeling like they can report it  quickly  number two the idea that if they report  it that they may not be taken seriously  or that there will be social  repercussions and other repercussions if  we could work on that we might be able  to reduce the severity of the problem of  people taking a long time to come  forward to tell the story of what  happened to them and part of this Pat  has to do with will they believe me will  it be will it be considered real rape or  is an  did I maybe do something wrong because  of the victim-blaming  yeah mentality that exists so I think  that there's a case to be made for  extending the statute of limitations on  sex crimes but I think there's other  stuff we have to tackle as well you know  what the average length is for the  statute of limitations I'd imagine it  depends on what state it depends on the  state I think sometimes it's as short as  a few years and sometimes it can be as  long as could it be as long as ten years  in some places maybe even 15 yeah and I  mean sure we can still litigate it in  the court of public opinion I mean take  a look at these what eight allegations  against Senate candidate Roy Moore yes  they were from 40 years ago okay but it  has to do with him becoming a u.s.  senator that's important now there's  other ways so the even without extending  the statute of limitations there's still  ways in which these allegations can have  an impact modern day and it hats right  about that but I want to mention  something else to consider what about a  statute that is dependent on the most  recent victim in other words if someone  is accused of having sexually assaulted  others over a 20-year period and the  most recent one is a year ago what if  you're allowed to look at all of the  alleged incidents of sexual assault even  if some of them individually might be  beyond the statute of limitations so for  example with Roy Moore if someone had  said he did this to me last year that  would trigger being able to prosecute  criminally all alleged instances of  sexual assault or something like that  right that's another idea that could be  considered sounds right to me and of  course I'm not a lawyer well by the way  Pat has to remind us on a regular basis  that he's not a lawyer so I'm glad you  mentioned that and I guess I'll take  this as an opportunity to say I'm not a  lawyer either nor would anyone hire me  no and he's not marketing himself as a  lawyer either please the issue with  indefinite right like no statute of  limitations is that for a guilty verdict  you do need to be sure beyond a  reasonable doubt that the person is  guilty and you can make a case after 30  years but the amount of time that has  passed in and of itself could constitute  what we might say as reasonable doubt no  matter how strongly the Witnesses  believe they remember the details and I  think that that's something that  needs to be considered yeah especially  if there are like scientific  breakthroughs that come about like you  know DNA testing did in the 1990s that  could you know either convict or  exonerate someone of that crime no doubt  no doubt about it alright those are my  thoughts on that one

